Adding root-cause analysis and a workaround after digging through the opencode source.

## Root cause: the `modalities` field is required but undocumented

Capability detection for image input lives in `packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts`, roughly:

```ts
capabilities: {
  input: {
    image: model.modalities?.input?.includes("image") ?? false,
    // ...
  }
}
```

If `modalities` is not defined on a custom model entry in `opencode.json`, `capabilities.input.image` defaults to `false`, and image parts are stripped **client-side** before the request is built. The model then receives text only and often replies literally "I don't support image input", which looks like a backend capability issue but is actually opencode's own gating check.

This explains why **the same backend works with a direct `image_url` curl** but fails through opencode — the `file` → `image_url` mapping logic is never reached, because the attachment is already gone by then.

## Minimal repro

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "my-kimi": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://your-openai-compatible-gateway/v1",
        "apiKey": "sk-..."
      },
      "models": {
        "kimi-k2.5": {
          "name": "Kimi K2.5"
          // no `modalities` → image gets stripped client-side
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Attach an image, send → assistant responds "I don't support image input."

## Workaround: declare `modalities` explicitly

```jsonc
"kimi-k2.5": {
  "name": "Kimi K2.5",
  "modalities": {
    "input": ["text", "image"],
    "output": ["text"]
  }
}
```

Restart opencode (the capability cache is built at startup, not hot-reloaded). Images now flow through correctly, verified end-to-end with Kimi K2.5 and K2.6 multimodal responses.

## Suggestions

1. **Document `modalities`** under the "Custom provider" section at `opencode.ai/docs/providers`. It's currently referenced nowhere in user-facing docs, even though it's the only switch that enables vision for custom providers.
2. **Tighten the `opencode.json` JSON schema** so `provider.*.models.*` describes `modalities`, `reasoning`, `toolCall`, `temperature`, etc. explicitly. Right now the inner model-entry shape is unconstrained, so IDE completion and schema validation give users zero hint that `modalities` exists.
3. **Optional UX**: when an image attachment is stripped because the model lacks `modalities.input.image`, surface a warning. That would turn a confusing symptom ("model says it doesn't support images") into an actionable one ("opencode stripped your image because `modalities.input` doesn't include 'image'").

Happy to open a docs PR for the first suggestion above.

> could you share the exact supported fields

The field is `modalities` on each model entry. Capability detection in `packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts` reads it as:

```ts
capabilities.input.image = model.modalities?.input?.includes("image") ?? false
```

If absent (the default when users register a custom model), `capabilities.input.image` evaluates to `false` and opencode strips image attachments client-side before the request is sent — which matches your "classified as text-only / blocks image input" symptom exactly.

Minimal config to unblock vision for your LiteLLM-backed models:

```jsonc
"provider": {
  "litellm": {
    "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
    "options": { "baseURL": "https://your-litellm-gateway/v1" },
    "models": {
      "claude-sonnet-4-6": {
        "modalities": { "input": ["text", "image"], "output": ["text"] }
      },
      "gpt-5.3-codex": {
        "modalities": { "input": ["text", "image"], "output": ["text"] },
        "reasoning": true
      },
      "deepseek-v3.2": {
        "modalities": { "input": ["text"], "output": ["text"] }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Restart opencode (capability cache is built once at startup, not hot-reloaded).

See #20802 for the full root-cause writeup — this is the same gating check manifesting across every custom `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` provider.
